<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austro-Hungarian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Austria-Hungary, Dual Monarchy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austro-Hungarian Empire, or Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, was a dual monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> and ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> and his successor. It combined many nationalities, whose tensions strained its political system. After the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-franz-ferdinand-of-austria">Franz Ferdinand</a> it went to war in 1914, and it dissolved in 1918 at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire.jpg" alt="Austro-Hungarian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Austria-Hungary_(1914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The administrative, legal and military institutions of this period, including the General Civil Code of 1811, passed to <b>Austria-Hungary</b> and, in part, to the successor states of central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Czechoslovakia was created as <b>Austria-Hungary</b> collapsed at the end of the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its central problem, how to govern many peoples with distinct laws and languages under one dynasty, passed to the Austrian Empire and <b>Austria-Hungary</b>, and still informs debates about federalism and nationality in Central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 October 1918, as <b>Austria-Hungary</b> collapsed, the Czechoslovak state was proclaimed in Prague, and the kingdom became the core of the new republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Abandoning the Triple Alliance with Germany and <b>Austria-Hungary</b>, Italy joined the Entente under the Treaty of London of April 1915 and entered the First World War that May.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Serbia, which had fought <b>Austria-Hungary</b> since 1914 and was occupied in 1915, sought to unite the Serbs and their kin, while South Slav politicians from the Habsburg lands, organised in exile as the Yugoslav Committee under Ante Trumbić, wanted a union of equals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
